    "text": "{\n  \"semantic_type\": \"dimension\",\n  \"unit\": null,\n  \"additive_over_entities\": null,\n  \"additive_over_time\": null,\n  \"time_rollup\": null,\n  \"valid_methods\": [],\n  \"period_basis\": null,\n  \"derivation\": null,\n  \"calendar\": null,\n  \"seasonal_adjustment\": null,\n  \"annualized\": null,\n  \"entity_grain\": null,\n  \"hierarchy\": null,\n  \"comparability\": null,\n  \"lineage\": {\n    \"source\": \"SEC EDGAR XBRL companyfacts API\",\n    \"url_template\": \"https://data.sec.gov/api/xbrl/companyfacts/CIK{cik}.json\",\n    \"retrieved_on\": \"2026-10-24\"\n  },\n  \"description\": \"Label column in the long-format fundamentals table that says which financial metric each row's value holds. It has seven values: capex, diluted_eps, net_income, op_cash_flow, operating_income, revenue and total_assets. It is a categorical key, not a measure, so it should never be aggregated. Always filter or group by it before aggregating value. The XBRL concept behind a label can vary by company (for example, DRI's op_cash_flow maps to the ContinuingOperations concept), so check the concept column when comparing across companies. The aggregation behavior of the value column depends on the metric. revenue, net_income, operating_income, op_cash_flow and capex are period flows in USD. diluted_eps is a per-share ratio in USD/shares and cannot be summed across companies. total_assets is a point-in-time balance (instant). Rows also mix period bases (Q, YTD6, FY, instant), so select one period basis before combining values over time.\"\n}",
